---
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  cppcoreguidelines-*,
  misc-*,
  modernize-*,
  performance-*,
  readability-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result,
  -bugprone-narrowing-conversions,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-c-arrays,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-magic-numbers,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-non-const-global-variables,
  -cppcoreguidelines-macro-usage,
  -cppcoreguidelines-narrowing-conversions,
  -cppcoreguidelines-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-array-to-pointer-decay,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-constant-array-index,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-pointer-arithmetic,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-cstyle-cast,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-reinterpret-cast,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-union-access,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-vararg,
  -misc-confusable-identifiers,
  -misc-no-recursion,
  -misc-use-internal-linkage,
  -modernize-avoid-c-arrays,
  -modernize-use-designated-initializers,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -readability-braces-around-statements,
  -readability-else-after-return,
  -readability-function-cognitive-complexity,
  -readability-function-size,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-implicit-bool-conversion,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-math-missing-parentheses,
  -readability-qualified-auto,
  -readability-uppercase-literal-suffix,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -misc-use-anonymous-namespace,
  -misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-do-while,
  -readability-avoid-unconditional-preprocessor-if,
  -bugprone-unhandled-exception-at-new,
  -bugprone-macro-parentheses,
  -readability-redundant-declaration,
  -bugprone-reserved-identifier,
  -modernize-use-std-numbers,
  -modernize-use-default-member-init,
  -cppcoreguidelines-use-default-member-init

# Checks switched off because they conflict with deliberate choices in this codebase rather
# than flagging defects:
#   misc-include-cleaner            - demands a direct #include for build-system macros (SAME70),
#                                     eCv annotations (_ecv_array) and toolchain typedefs; the
#                                     include chain here is deliberate and order-sensitive
#   misc-use-anonymous-namespace    - file-scope `static` is the established RepRapFirmware idiom
#   misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes - duplicate of the cppcoreguidelines check
#                                     already disabled above
#   cppcoreguidelines-avoid-do-while - do/while(0) is the standard multi-statement macro idiom
#   readability-avoid-unconditional-preprocessor-if - `#if 0` guards debug-only code on purpose
#   bugprone-unhandled-exception-at-new - the firmware runs without exceptions; allocation
#                                     failure is handled by the fault path, not a catch
#   readability-redundant-declaration - the forward declarations of the fault handlers in
#                                     ExceptionHandlers.cpp are not redundant: they carry
#                                     __attribute__((naked)) and (noreturn) that the definitions
#                                     below them do not repeat, and the check deletes the whole
#                                     declaration. Losing 'naked' makes GCC wrap hand-written
#                                     assembly that reads MSP/PSP and r0/r1 in a prologue and
#                                     epilogue. It happens to still work on GCC 15 -- the emitted
#                                     epilogue is unreachable and no prologue is needed -- but the
#                                     asm is only correct because of the attribute, and nothing
#                                     would warn if a future compiler decided to save a register.
#   modernize-use-default-member-init (and its cppcoreguidelines- alias, which has to be named
#                                     separately or the wildcard re-enables it) - the diagnostic is
#                                     sound but its fix-it produces code that does not compile, in
#                                     two ways, across 9 of the constructors here:
#                                       * when every initialiser moves to the declaration it deletes
#                                         them but leaves the dangling ':', e.g. "RepRap() noexcept
#                                         :\n{". That is a clang-tidy bug, not a property of this
#                                         codebase.
#                                       * its replacement ranges span preprocessor directives that
#                                         are inactive in the configuration being analysed. In
#                                         DataTransfer's constructor it deleted the whole
#                                         "#if SAME5x ... #endif" arm -- initialisers for an MCU
#                                         family this build never compiles -- and orphaned the
#                                         "#endif" of the SUPPORTS_SBC_OVER_USB block.
#                                     The second one silently removes code for other targets, so
#                                     re-enable this only with a per-file review of the fix-it
#                                     rather than a bulk clang-apply-replacements run.
#   modernize-use-std-numbers       - it reports these as exact ("differs by 0.00e+00"), but that
#                                     is measured without -fsingle-precision-constant, which the
#                                     firmware does build with and Scripts/sanitise-compile-
#                                     commands.py has to drop because clang rejects it. Under the
#                                     real flags 180.0 / 3.141592653589793 folds in single
#                                     precision and std::numbers::pi (a double) does not, so
#                                     RadiansToDegrees moves one ulp: 0x42652ee0 -> 0x42652ee1.
#                                     The constants in RepRapFirmware.h are all constexpr, so
#                                     there is no runtime benefit to trade against changing a
#                                     motion constant.
#   bugprone-macro-parentheses      - the only hits are the build's own command-line -D flags
#                                     (-DMQTTC_PAL_FILE=Networking/MQTT/mqtt_pal.h); it fires once
#                                     per TU with no source location, and parenthesising that
#                                     value would break the `#include MQTTC_PAL_FILE` that uses it

# Known residue, left enabled on purpose. Three checks report hits that cannot be fixed or
# suppressed from this repository, all of them generated by the NamedEnum macro in
# lib/RRFLibraries/src/General/NamedEnum.h and attributed to its two use sites -- the Module enum in
# RepRapFirmware.h and BoardState in ExpansionManager.h:
#   cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer  ("v = arg" in the generated constructor)
#   cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions   (generated copy ctor + copy assignment, no dtor)
#   misc-unconventional-assign-operator          (generated operator= does not return a reference;
#                                                 also reported as its alias
#                                                 cppcoreguidelines-c-copy-assignment-signature)
# The fix would have to be made in lib/, which Duet3Expansion shares, and a NOLINT at the use site
# does not help: clang-tidy matches NOLINT against the spelling location inside the macro definition
# rather than the expansion. All three stay enabled because they do find real cases in first-party
# code -- between them they produced the CanDriversData, MemoryWatcher, IoPort, OutputBuffer,
# OutputStack, Platform, RepRap and AutoClearingWatchpoint fixes.

WarningsAsErrors: ''

# Anchored to this project's own first-party sources. Two things are deliberately excluded:
#   - lib/{CANlib,CoreN2G,FreeRTOS,LibTinyusb,RRFLibraries}/src/, which a bare 'src/.*' would
#     match. Duet3Expansion compiles those same headers from this checkout, so fixing them here
#     would silently change a project that is out of scope for this style guide.
#   - src/libc/ and src/libcpp/, which are vendored newlib and GCC runtime sources (FSF
#     copyright); they are upstream code and must keep upstream formatting.
# clang-tidy 18 has no ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex and its regex engine has no negative lookahead,
# so the first-party directories are enumerated instead.
#
# The leading alternation is load-bearing. clang-tidy matches this against the path exactly as it
# appears in the diagnostic, which for a database generated in this directory is relative --
# 'src/CAN/CanDriversData.h'. An absolute '/src/DuetCANMaster/src/' prefix therefore matches
# nothing and silently suppresses every header warning; a bare unanchored 'src/' matches too much
# (51 headers under lib/*/src/ -- lib/CANlib/src/CanId.h and friends -- satisfy the '[A-Za-z]+\.h'
# alternative). '^' covers the relative form, '/DuetCANMaster/' the absolute one, and neither can
# match anything under lib/.
HeaderFilterRegex: '(^|/DuetCANMaster/)src/((CAN|Config|Hardware|Movement|Platform|SBC|Storage)/.*|[A-Za-z]+\.h)$'

# The firmware is GCC-built; clang hits a handful of constexpr/overload divergences it cannot
# resolve. Without this it stops at 20 errors and silently leaves the rest of the TU unanalysed.
ExtraArgs: ['-ferror-limit=0']

FormatStyle: file

CheckOptions:
  - key: modernize-loop-convert.MinConfidence
    value: reasonable
  - key: modernize-use-nullptr.NullMacros
    value: 'NULL'
  - key: performance-move-const-arg.CheckTriviallyCopyableMove
    value: 'false'
  # These classes are singletons (Platform, RepRap), pooled objects (OutputBuffer, OutputStack) or
  # RAII owners (IoPort, MemoryWatcher, AutoClearingWatchpoint). Deleting the copy operations is the
  # whole intent -- demanding a move constructor and move assignment on top of that would mean
  # declaring two more deleted members per class for no benefit.
  - key: cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions.AllowMissingMoveFunctionsWhenCopyIsDeleted
    value: 'true'
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.FunctionCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumConstantCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.VariableCase
    value: camelBack
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstantCase
    value: camelBack
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ParameterCase
    value: camelBack
  # Symbols whose spelling is ABI, not style. Renaming these compiles cleanly and then fails
  # at link time -- or worse, silently changes a vector-table slot:
  #   VersionText     - CoreN2G's SAME70 Vectors.c stores its address in pfnReserved2_Handler
  #   exception_table - CoreN2G's DeviceVectors instance
  #   *_Handler       - CMSIS interrupt vector names, matched by the startup vector table
  #   _*              - the whole leading-underscore class is reserved for the toolchain, and the
  #                     linker resolves these by exact spelling against the runtime rather than
  #                     against anything in this tree: _estack (same70q20b_flash.ld),
  #                     __cxa_pure_virtual / __cxa_deleted_virtual (emitted by GCC for a call to a
  #                     pure or deleted virtual) and __cxxabiv1::__terminate_handler (the hook that
  #                     replaces libstdc++'s default). Renaming any of them still links, because
  #                     libstdc++ quietly supplies its own -- so the firmware's SoftwareReset
  #                     diagnostics are bypassed at runtime and __verbose_terminate_handler gets
  #                     pulled in, which is exactly the bloat ExceptionHandlers.cpp avoids.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalVariableIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^(_.*|VersionText|exception_table)$'
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^(_.*|VersionText|exception_table|DiagPin|ActLedPin|DiagOnPolarity)$'
  # DiagPin / ActLedPin / DiagOnPolarity are spelled identically on purpose so that the shared
  # code in Tasks.cpp and Platform.cpp compiles for both boards, even though the entity behind
  # the name differs per target:
  #   MB6XD - global constexpr constants in Pins_Duet3_MB6XD.h
  #   MB6HC - Platform members (Platform.h, #ifdef DUET3_MB6HC) and locals in AppMain(), because
  #           the pins depend on the board revision and are only known at runtime
  # Each board therefore renames these under a different rule (m_ prefix for the member, camelBack
  # for the local) while leaving the other board's spelling untouched, and the merged result
  # satisfies neither. The shared spelling is a portability contract, not a style lapse.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^(DiagPin|ActLedPin|DiagOnPolarity)$'
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.LocalConstantIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^(DiagPin|ActLedPin|DiagOnPolarity)$'
  # *Dispatcher - referenced only from the inline-asm literals in ExceptionHandlers.cpp
  #               (" handler_hf_address_const: .word hardFaultDispatcher"). clang-tidy renames the
  #               C++ definition but cannot see inside an asm string, so the reference dangles and
  #               the link fails with "undefined reference".
  # debugPrintf - imported by the prebuilt lib/CANlib/SAME70_RTOS/libCANlib.a, which is not built
  #               from this tree. Its spelling is a link-time contract, not a style choice; it is
  #               the only one of the 272 renamed identifiers that any prebuilt archive imports.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.FunctionIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^(_.*|.*_Handler|.*Dispatcher|debugPrintf|vAssertCalled|atoi)$'
  # 'pwm' and 'spi' CamelCase to Pwm/Spi, which are peripheral struct typedefs in the Atmel CMSIS
  # headers (component/pwm.h, component/spi.h). PinAccess and PinCapability are scoped enums, so
  # the enumerators cannot actually be confused with those globals -- but GCC's -Wshadow reports
  # scoped enumerators anyway and the firmware builds with -Werror, so the rename breaks the build.
  # These two are the only new identifiers out of 272 that collide with a CMSIS global.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumConstantIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^(pwm|spi)$'
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberPrefix
    value: 'm_'
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.StaticVariablePrefix
    value: 's_'
